        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">120</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">teontig <hi rend="italic">So</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">teontig So</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Hier sollte neues Kapitel beginnen. — Über 10 Hufen <hi rend="italic">vgl. o.</hi> 65</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">A new chapter ought to begin here. — On 10 hides <hi rend="italic">cf. above</hi> 65</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht ‘Nährlohn für Kinder’ (Thorpe). Vielmehr ‘Fruchtzins, Rente eines Herrschaftsguts’, durch viele Bauern aufgebracht und durch den Vogt eingesammelt, aber nicht allein für den König fällig (Phillipps <hi rend="italic">Agsä. Rg.</hi> S. 87), auch nicht statt Gastung (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 4. 12) einer Nacht (Robertson <hi rend="italic">Histor. essays</hi> 107) oder nur wenn der Herr durchs Vassallenland reist (Ducange s. v. <hi rend="italic">corredium</hi>). — In Urkk. ist <hi rend="italic">fostorland</hi> (Toller; a. 934 Bi 738) ‘Tafelgut, zum Unterhalt des Herrn dienend’, im Ggs. zu Holz- und Kleiderland (<hi rend="italic">scrudland;</hi> Toller). — Ähnliche Listen von Naturalabgaben: Kemble <hi rend="italic">Saxons</hi> I 315; Urkk. Bi 272. 403. 405. 412; die Periodizität als jährliche erwähnen Bi 330. 452. 464; Kemble 695. Meist bleibt sie wie hier als selbstverständlich unausgedrückt. — <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Brot, Schaf, Gans, Huhn, Käse, Lachs, Aal. [Die Südsachsen kannten allein Aalfischerei, bevor 681/6 Wilfrid Seefischerei lehrte; Beda IV 13]</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not ‘payment for nursing children’ (Thorpe). Rather ‘food-rent, the revenue of a manorial estate’, raised by many peasants and collected by the reeve, but not due to the king alone (Phillipps <hi rend="italic">Agsä. Rg.</hi> p. 87), nor in place of a night’s hospitality (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 4. 12) (Robertson <hi rend="italic">Histor. essays</hi> 107), nor only when the lord travels through the vassal’s land (Ducange s. v. <hi rend="italic">corredium</hi>). — In charters <hi rend="italic">fostorland</hi> (Toller; a. 934 Bi 738) is ‘table-land, serving for the lord’s maintenance’, as opposed to wood-land and clothing-land (<hi rend="italic">scrudland;</hi> Toller). — Similar lists of renders in kind: Kemble <hi rend="italic">Saxons</hi> I 315; charters Bi 272. 403. 405. 412; their periodicity as annual is mentioned in Bi 330. 452. 464; Kemble 695. Mostly, as here, it remains unexpressed as self-evident. — <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Brot, Schaf, Gans, Huhn, Käse, Lachs, Aal. [The South Saxons knew only eel-fishing before Wilfrid taught them sea-fishing in 681/6; Beda IV 13]</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Bienen 2. Um 815 gilt Honig doppelt so hoch als Wein; Urk. Bi 330</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Bienen 2. About 815 honey is valued twice as highly as wine; charter Bi 330</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Eimer. Er steht = ¹/₂ <hi rend="italic">mitta;</hi> Toller <hi rend="italic">s. v.;</hi> der Deutsche misst meist 64—69 Liter, in Württemberg 293. Auch Malz und Bierwürze mass man danach; Urkk. Bi 405. 412. — Zum Bier <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3 b</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Eimer. It equals ¹/₂ <hi rend="italic">mitta;</hi> Toller <hi rend="italic">s. v.;</hi> the German one usually measures 64–69 litres, in Württemberg 293. Malt and wort were also measured by it; charters Bi 405. 412. — On beer <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3 b</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Rinder u. a. Tiere in Urkk. häufig (Bi 403. 405): <hi rend="italic">ealdhriðer</hi> Bi 403</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Cattle and other animals frequent in charters (Bi 403. 405): <hi rend="italic">ealdhriðer</hi> Bi 403</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Butter</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Butter</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_70-1.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">81</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Statt ‘Wispel’ setze ‘Schwergewicht, drei kleine Wispel, 35—53 Zentner’; <hi rend="italic">vgl. o.</hi> 59, 1⁵</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">For ‘Wispel’ read ‘heavy weight, three small Wispel, 35–53 hundredweight’; <hi rend="italic">cf. above</hi> 59, 1⁵</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.attnote.fn.10" n="10" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope></bibl><label>hriðeru</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">twa ealda ryðeru</hi>. B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.attnote.fn.11" n="11" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope></bibl><label>x</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">⁊. B*.</seg><note type="remark" resp="#claude">The number 11 is printed 2 times on the page (70.1, 70.1), with one footnote.</note></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.attnote.fn.11-2" n="11" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope></bibl><label>x</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">⁊. B*.</seg><note type="remark" resp="#claude">The number 11 is printed 2 times on the page (70.1, 70.1), with one footnote.</note></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.attnote.fn.12" n="12" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope></bibl><label>xx</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">⁊. H*. (⁊ <hi rend="italic">tyn gees</hi> ⁊ <hi rend="italic">twenti henna</hi> ⁊ <hi rend="italic">tyn cysas</hi>.) B*.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">192</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The amber was apparently half a mitta, but in neither case can the capacity be determined at this time. In the 13th century the amber contained 4 bushels (see Harmer, <hi rend="italic">op. cit.</hi> p. 73).</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.attnote.n.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">192</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Similar payments are not unfrequently mentioned in charters (see Harmer, <hi rend="italic">Historical Documents</hi>, <hi rend="smallcaps">i</hi>. <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi>. etc.; Maitland, <hi rend="italic">Domesday Book and Beyond</hi>, pp. 234, 318, 324) In Birch, <hi rend="italic">Cart.</hi> <hi rend="italic">Sax.</hi> 1010, a payment very much less than this is described as one day’s <hi rend="italic">feorm</hi> for Christchurch, Canterbury, in the middle of the 10th century.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_70-1.whinote.n.4" n="4" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">407</biblScope></bibl><label>pounds</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Liebermann translates “Wispel”, a German measure of about 24 bushels.</seg></note>
